Abstract

This document defines the communication model for the Memory Operating System.

The Communication Model describes how Cognitive Systems exchange information, coordinate actions, delegate responsibilities, synchronize knowledge, and establish trust.

Communication in MOS is not treated as message passing alone.

Messages represent cognitive interactions.

Every communication event SHALL preserve:

identity,
provenance,
intent,
authority,
context,
trust,
lifecycle.

This allows MOS to support:

multi-agent systems,
distributed cognition,
human-agent collaboration,
organizational intelligence,
networked cognitive systems.
1. Purpose

Previous RFCs describe cognition inside a single Cognitive State Space.

However, real intelligent systems will not always exist as one process.

Future MOS deployments may contain:

multiple agents,
multiple machines,
edge devices,
cloud systems,
human operators,
specialized cognitive nodes.

Therefore:

Cognition must be able to communicate without losing semantic meaning.

2. Communication Axiom

MOS defines:

Every cognitive interaction SHALL be represented as a Cognitive Communication Event.

Communication SHALL NOT be treated as anonymous data transfer.

3. Cognitive Network Model

MOS introduces the concept of Cognitive Nodes.

A Cognitive Node is an independent cognitive participant.

Examples:

AI agent
human user
robot
service
organization
specialized model

A Cognitive Network:

              Cognitive Network


        Node A -------- Node B

          │              │

          │              │

        Node C -------- Node D

Each node may possess:

memory,
capabilities,
policies,
resources,
identity.
4. Cognitive Node

A Node is a first-class Cognitive Element.

A Node contains:

Node

Identity

Capabilities

Resources

Authority

Trust Profile

Communication Interface

Provenance

Example:

Node:

Research Agent

Capabilities:

Web Search
Analysis
Summarization


Resources:

GPU Available


Trust:

Verified
5. Cognitive Communication Event

Communication creates immutable Events.

A Communication Event contains:

Communication Event

Sender

Receiver

Timestamp

Intent

Message Type

Payload

Authority

Trust Context

Provenance

Response State
6. Message Types

MOS defines several semantic message categories.

6.1 Request

A request asks another node to perform a cognitive action.

Example:

Request:

Capability:
Analyze Document

Intent:
Extract Financial Risk

Priority:
High
6.2 Response

Provides results from a request.

Contains:

result
confidence
provenance
limitations
6.3 Proposal

Suggests an action.

Example:

"Optimize retrieval strategy."

6.4 Delegation

Transfers responsibility.

Example:

Agent A

delegates

Market Analysis

to

Financial Agent B
6.5 Synchronization

Shares cognitive state updates.

Example:

new facts
changed policies
learned strategies
6.6 Observation Sharing

Provides external observations.

Example:

Sensor → Agent.

7. Communication Intent

Every communication SHALL have an associated Intent.

Example:

Bad:

Send data

Good:

Intent:

Improve market prediction accuracy

Communication:

Send financial report analysis
8. Context Preservation

Communication SHALL preserve relevant context.

Context MAY include:

previous decisions,
related entities,
active goals,
constraints,
policies.
9. Delegation Model

Delegation allows one cognitive system to assign responsibility.

A delegation SHALL define:

Delegating Node

Receiving Node

Assigned Capability

Authority Level

Constraints

Expected Outcome

Expiration
10. Authority Model

Communication requires authority.

Authority determines:

what a node may request,
what a node may modify,
what information may be shared.

Authority is governed by Governance Policies from RFC-0004.

11. Trust Model

Communication SHALL include trust information.

Trust considers:

identity verification,
historical reliability,
previous outcomes,
confidence.

Trust is not binary.

Example:

Agent:

Financial Analyst

Trust:

0.91
12. Knowledge Exchange

Knowledge exchange SHALL preserve:

source,
creator,
confidence,
timestamp,
original context.

A received fact SHALL NOT automatically become trusted knowledge.

Flow:

External Knowledge

↓

Observation

↓

Validation

↓

Integration

↓

Knowledge
13. Cognitive Synchronization

Distributed nodes MAY synchronize cognition.

Synchronization types:

Full Synchronization

Share complete cognitive state.

Partial Synchronization

Share selected domains.

Event Synchronization

Share only changes.

Semantic Synchronization

Share meaning rather than raw data.

14. Conflict Resolution

Distributed cognition creates conflicts.

Example:

Agent A:

"Company revenue increased."

Agent B:

"Company revenue decreased."

MOS SHALL preserve both claims.

Resolution occurs through:

provenance comparison,
confidence,
source reliability,
temporal analysis,
evaluation.
15. Network Cognitive Policies

Communication Policies are Meta-Cognitive Elements.

Examples:

Privacy Policy

Controls information sharing.

Trust Policy

Controls accepted sources.

Cost Policy

Controls communication expense.

Latency Policy

Controls response requirements.

16. Resource-Aware Communication

RFC-0008 integrates with RFC-0007.

Communication consumes:

bandwidth,
compute,
storage,
time.

The Orchestrator MAY choose:

local processing,
remote delegation,
cached knowledge,
delayed synchronization.
17. Cognitive Protocol Stack

MOS communication architecture:

Application Intent

        │

        ▼

Capability Request

        │

        ▼

Cognitive Message

        │

        ▼

Communication Event

        │

        ▼

Network Transport

        │

